/* ---------------- Article 15: Data silos ---------------- */
const silosSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  { id: "what", title: "What is a data silo?", answer: "A data silo is information held inside one system, department or workflow that cannot be reliably combined with the information needed elsewhere. Separate systems are not the problem in themselves; the problem is when required information cannot move or be reconciled.", paragraphs: ["Most organizations run several applications for good reasons: a CRM for customer relationships, an ERP for financial transactions, an operational platform for jobs or shipments, specialist tools for particular teams. Specialization is healthy. A silo becomes costly when a business question needs information from several of those systems and the answer requires manual exports, reconciliation meetings or guesswork.", "So the test is practical: which decisions depend on cross-system information, and how dependable is the path to it? That framing avoids the false conclusion that every independent tool must be replaced."] },
  { id: "visibility-gap", title: "The Xyncwave Visibility Gap", variant: "framework", answer: "CRM, ERP, operations, finance and spreadsheets all feed a business question. Between them sit different definitions, manual exports, duplicate records, timing differences, missing identifiers and inconsistent rules. The result is a delayed, disputed or incomplete answer.", paragraphs: ["A business can own plenty of data and still lack reliable information. The Visibility Gap names what lies between systems and the question: the reasons the same figure differs depending on who prepared it. Closing the gap means addressing those specific causes, not simply collecting data in one place.", "Use it with a real question—such as 'what did it cost to serve our top customers last quarter?'—and list which systems contribute and which gap factors apply. That list becomes the work plan."] },
  { id: "why", title: "Why data silos appear", answer: "Silos usually result from reasonable decisions made over time: departments choosing tools, organic growth, acquisitions, new locations, ERP plus specialist applications, spreadsheets, legacy systems, missing integrations and differing definitions.", paragraphs: ["Each decision was often sensible when made. A team adopted a tool that fit its work. An acquisition brought its own systems. A new location set up a spreadsheet to cope. Nobody designed the silos; they accumulated because nobody was responsible for how information connected across the whole business.", "That matters for the response. Blaming departments or tools rarely helps. Assigning ownership for cross-system information, and deciding which system is authoritative for what, does."] },
  { id: "five-forms", title: "The five forms of data fragmentation", answer: "System fragmentation (separate applications), department fragmentation (separate processes and definitions), format fragmentation (different structures), timing fragmentation (different update cycles) and semantic fragmentation (different meanings for the same term).", subsections: [
    { title: "System fragmentation", paragraphs: ["Data lives in separate applications without dependable exchange. Remedy: integration or data pipelines, depending on whether the need is operational or analytical."] },
    { title: "Department fragmentation", paragraphs: ["Teams maintain separate processes, records and definitions for related work. Remedy: agree ownership and shared definitions before building connections."] },
    { title: "Format fragmentation", paragraphs: ["Files, spreadsheets, APIs and databases use different structures and identifiers. Remedy: standardization and mapping as part of a controlled pipeline."] },
    { title: "Timing fragmentation", paragraphs: ["Systems update at different times, so figures legitimately differ depending on when they are read. Remedy: state data freshness explicitly and align update cycles where it matters."] },
    { title: "Semantic fragmentation", paragraphs: ["Different teams mean different things by the same metric—'active customer', 'completed order', 'revenue'. Remedy: a governed business glossary with owners for each term."] },
  ], paragraphs: ["Most visibility problems involve more than one form. Semantic fragmentation is the one most often missed, because the data appears consistent until two reports disagree."] },
  { id: "dashboards", title: "Why dashboards often fail to solve the real problem", answer: "A dashboard can only present the quality and consistency of its underlying data. If source systems disagree, visualization presents inconsistent information more attractively.", paragraphs: ["Organizations frequently respond to poor visibility by commissioning dashboards. The first review meeting then becomes a debate about why the dashboard's number differs from finance's spreadsheet. The dashboard did not create the disagreement; it exposed it.", "Dashboards are valuable once definitions, identifiers and data flows are dependable. Before that point, effort is better spent on the pipeline and definitions behind a small number of important figures."] },
  { id: "one-system", title: "The myth of \"one system for everything\"", answer: "Businesses do not need to replace every tool. Multiple specialized systems can work well with clear ownership, integration, consistent identifiers, defined data models and reliable pipelines.", paragraphs: ["Consolidating onto one platform can remove some silos, but it brings migration effort, adoption risk and compromises for teams whose work the platform fits poorly. It can also create new spreadsheets when the single system cannot handle a specialist process.", "Consolidation makes sense where tools are genuinely redundant. Elsewhere, connecting well-chosen systems is usually less disruptive and preserves the fit each team needs."], links: [{ label: "System integration strategy", to: "/insights/system-integration-strategy" }, { label: "ERP vs custom software", to: "/insights/erp-vs-custom-software" }] },
  { id: "integration-vs-data", title: "Integration vs data engineering", answer: "System integration moves operational information between applications so work can proceed. Data engineering builds reliable data flows for analytics, reporting and consumption. Many visibility problems need both.", subsections: [
    { title: "System integration", paragraphs: ["Used when one system must act on another's information promptly—an order confirmed in the CRM creating a job in operations, or a completed job triggering billing. Concerns: ownership, timing, failure handling, retries."] },
    { title: "Data engineering", paragraphs: ["Used when information from several systems must be combined, standardized and validated for reporting or analysis. Concerns: ingestion, transformation, validation, traceability, history."] },
  ], links: [{ label: "API & System Integration", to: "/solutions/integration" }, { label: "Data Engineering", to: "/solutions/data-engineering" }] },
  { id: "decision-map", title: "The Silo Resolution Decision Map", variant: "framework", answer: "For each visibility problem ask five questions: Is immediate operational action required? Is the goal reporting or analytics? Is the underlying process duplicated? Are tools redundant? Is the existing system fundamentally limiting? Each answer points to integration, a data pipeline, process redesign, consolidation or modernization.", table: { headers: ["Question", "If yes", "Likely direction"], rows: [["Is operational action required immediately?", "Information must reach another system to proceed", "Integration"], ["Is the goal reporting or analytics?", "Information must be combined and trusted", "Data pipeline / warehouse"], ["Is the underlying process duplicated?", "Two teams do the same work differently", "Process redesign"], ["Are multiple tools redundant?", "Systems overlap in purpose", "Consolidation"], ["Is the existing system fundamentally limiting?", "It cannot expose or hold what is needed", "Modernization / replacement"]] }, paragraphs: ["A single problem can trigger more than one answer. Work through the questions in order and resist starting with the technology."] },
  { id: "symptoms", title: "Common symptoms of harmful data silos", bullets: ["Weekly spreadsheet consolidation before management meetings.", "Conflicting KPIs from different departments.", "Duplicate customer or vendor records.", "Manual finance reconciliation between systems.", "Delayed management reporting.", "Different status definitions in different teams.", "Repeated data exports for the same purpose.", "Manual copy-paste between systems.", "Difficulty tracing a reported number to its source."], paragraphs: ["If several of these recur around the same business question, that question is a good place to start."], links: [{ label: "Digital transformation starts with fragmentation", to: "/insights/digital-transformation-fragmented-systems" }] },
  { id: "single-source", title: "\"Single source of truth\" does not necessarily mean one database", answer: "Different systems can remain authoritative for different domains: CRM for customer relationships, ERP for financial transactions, an operational platform for job or shipment status, and a warehouse for the analytical model. The key is ownership and reconciliation.", paragraphs: ["A single source of truth is better understood as a single agreed source for each type of fact. When everyone knows which system owns customer contact details and which owns invoice totals, integrations and pipelines can be designed around that ownership, and disagreements can be resolved by reference rather than debate.", "An analytical warehouse then combines those authoritative sources into models for reporting. It does not replace them; it reconciles them. Shared definitions and named owners for key business terms make that reconciliation stable."], links: [{ label: "Data engineering for growing businesses", to: "/insights/data-engineering-growing-businesses" }] },
  { id: "logistics", title: "Data silos in logistics", answer: "Dispatch, warehouse, tracking, finance and reporting systems each hold part of the shipment story. Visibility depends on connecting status and cost across them.", paragraphs: ["A typical gap: dispatch knows what left, tracking knows where it is, the warehouse knows what was picked and finance knows what was invoiced—but nobody can quickly answer whether a delivered shipment has been billed correctly. Connecting operational status and establishing shared shipment identifiers is usually the first step."], links: [{ label: "Logistics digital transformation in Africa", to: "/insights/logistics-digital-transformation-africa" }, { label: "Track & Trace case study", to: "/case-studies/track-trace" }] },
  { id: "healthcare", title: "Data silos in healthcare", answer: "Registration, scheduling, departmental systems, billing and reporting often hold overlapping information about the same service, with different identifiers and update times.", paragraphs: ["The operational effect is familiar: re-entry at registration, manual confirmation of appointments, and reporting that requires consolidation before management can see activity across departments. The healthcare articles in this series cover the workflow and pipeline sides of that problem."], links: [{ label: "Healthcare digital transformation in Africa", to: "/insights/healthcare-digital-transformation-africa" }, { label: "Healthcare data pipeline modernization", to: "/insights/healthcare-data-pipeline-modernization" }] },
  { id: "manufacturing", title: "Data silos in manufacturing", answer: "Production, inventory, procurement, quality, maintenance, ERP and reporting frequently run on different systems and cycles, making questions about true cost or capacity hard to answer.", paragraphs: ["For example, understanding why output fell short in a given week may require production records, maintenance logs, material availability from procurement and quality holds—each in a different system with different item codes. Aligning identifiers and deciding which system owns each fact makes that question answerable without a week of spreadsheet work."], links: [{ label: "Manufacturing industry overview", to: "/industries/manufacturing" }] },
  { id: "start", title: "Where to start", answer: "Define the business question, identify source systems and the authoritative source, find manual reconciliation, standardize identifiers and definitions, decide between operational integration and an analytical pipeline, validate and expand iteratively.", bullets: ["01 Define the business question leadership cannot answer reliably.", "02 Identify the source systems involved.", "03 Identify the authoritative source for each fact.", "04 Find where manual reconciliation happens today.", "05 Standardize identifiers and definitions.", "06 Decide: operational integration, analytical pipeline or both.", "07 Validate results against a trusted reference.", "08 Expand to the next question iteratively."], paragraphs: ["Starting with one question keeps scope bounded and produces a visible result that builds support for the next step."], links: [{ label: "Digital Transformation", to: "/solutions/digital-transformation" }] },
  { id: "proof", title: "Practical proof", variant: "proof", paragraphs: ["Track & Trace describes connected operational systems for logistics visibility. The Snowflake + dbt Analytics Platform case describes structured data ingestion and transformation into analytical models. The Enterprise Reporting & Data Accuracy case describes making reporting data traceable from source to output.", "These cases document engineering scope. They do not quantify reporting-time reductions, accuracy percentages or financial outcomes, and none are claimed."], links: [{ label: "Track & Trace case study", to: "/case-studies/track-trace" }, { label: "Snowflake + dbt Analytics Platform", to: "/case-studies/snowflake-dbt-analytics-platform" }, { label: "Enterprise Reporting & Data Accuracy", to: "/case-studies/enterprise-reporting-data-accuracy" }] },
];
